Silt-Dock Grime
Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE HOUSE + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Clarinet
Creative Catalyst: The style was codified by grandmothers who DJ at a floating market before dawn.

It sounds like industrial bilge water whispering secrets to an infinite four-on-the-floor kick drum. Hyper-compressed progressive house foundations—massive, sidechained basslines and glittering, horizon-stretching pad sweeps—are aggressively subverted by the weeping, phenolic shriek of wet wooden clarinets forced through high-voltage guitar amplifiers. Beneath the relentless 128 BPM hydraulic pulse, microtonal finger-holes and dripping brass keys improvise weeping laments over the hum of rotting cargo hulls, while pitch-shifted octaves blur the line between a reedy acoustic instrument and a sputtering server rack coughing up damp binary code.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 126 to 132 BPM, driven by an unyielding progressive house four-on-the-floor kick, peppered with polyrhythmic damp-clap accents and organic water-slap percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark microtonal modes, dissonant overtone clusters, and cold industrial minor scales punctuated by warm, breathing acoustic timbres.
- Production Signatures: Hydro-sidechaining (where the bass ducks under the sound of splashing river water), copper-reed saturation, and endless algorithmic delay tails that smear clarinet arpeggios across sterile cybernetic landscapes.
- Dynamics: Immersive, tectonic builds that stretch for eight minutes, breaking open into surreal ambient drift sections where wet reeds echo across rusted steel shipping containers before the sub-bass re-ignites.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the humid, toxic labyrinth of the floating barge-bazaars at 4:30 AM, Silt-Dock Grime was accidentally invented by retired maritime market matriarchs testing ancient public address systems while bartering for fermented jellyfish. Adorned in oil-stained lace aprons over pneumatic cyber-skeletons, these grandmotherly selectors manipulate floating mixing desks built from salvaged titanium fruit crates and antique valve radios. Their subculture trades in bootlegged hydro-cables, dried kelp-tea, and fiercely loyal street politics, operating entirely in the foggy hour before the corporate drone-tugs begin their morning patrols.

Typical Instruments: Carbon-fiber Boehm-system clarinets with piezo-electric pickups, modular analog synthesizers housed in galvanized steel buckets, heavy 909-style drum machines submerged in mineral oil, and copper contact microphones clamped to floating wooden pylons.

For Fans Of: Four Tet, Mount Kimbie, Ben UFO, Portico Quartet.
